{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfyjoSEJy8iws2CnMmfqo","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"`--dry-run` (short form `-n`) makes rsync go through the whole transfer without actually doing it. It works out what it would copy, update or delete and shows you, but it doesn't write, change or delete any files on the destination.\n\nIt's usually paired with `-v` (verbose) or `--itemize-changes` (`-i`) so you can see what would happen:\n\n```bash\nrsync -avn source/ dest/\nrsync -ai --dry-run --delete source/ dest/\n```\n\n**Common uses:**\n- **Testing a risky command** before running it for real, especially with `--delete`, which removes files from the destination that aren't in the source.\n- **Checking trailing slashes:** `source/` copies the folder's contents, while `source` copies the folder itself. A dry run shows which one you'll get.\n- **Seeing what's different** between two directories without changing either one.\n\n**Caveats:**\n- It still connects to the remote host and scans both sides, so you need working credentials and it isn't instant on large trees.\n- The output is a very close preview, but not always exact. For example, files that change between the dry run and the real run will behave differently, and stats like \"bytes sent\" won't match a real transfer.\n\nOnce the output looks right, run the same command again without `-n`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12087,"cache_read_input_tokens":25083,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":12087},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ff01f16c-7b3f-5ea5-8051-7a95ac7cd1d1","uuid":"3c21cc0f-d9b5-4eb0-b917-f13fcf19e4a8","timestamp":"2026-10-13T06:10:03.112Z","re